            "entity:DeepSeek"
          ],
          "narration": "Three moves to your own fine-tuned Llama tonight. You watched DeepSeek V four point one land yesterday and wanted your own."
        },
        {
          "id": "s02",
          "role": "foreshadow",
          "est_duration_s": 13.8,
          "specific": true,
          "hits": [
            "num:about 125 GB of memory at 1-bit, or 300 GB at 4-bit",
            "entity:Unsloth",
            "entity:Training"
          ],
          "narration": "Here is the honest catch. Unsloth's new beta runs that model in about one hundred twenty-five gigabytes of memory. But support means running it. Running shipped; the training recipe stayed home. Fine-tuning, teaching it your own examples, shipped no recipe."
        },
        {
          "id": "s03",
          "role": "explain",
          "est_duration_s": 14.8,
          "specific": true,
          "hits": [
            "entity:Unsloth",
            "entity:Training"
          ],
          "narration": "So tonight you train the stock Unsloth recipe on a model that fits your card. Memory follows the whole checkpoint, every saved weight, not the slice that thinks. If a V four point one training path lands later, this same recipe inherits it."
        },
        {
          "id": "s04",
          "role": "explain",
          "est_duration_s": 11.7,
          "specific": true,
          "hits": [
            "num:just 3GB VRAM",
            "entity:Unsloth",
            "entity:Python"
          ],
          "narration": "Move one: install Unsloth with one pasted command. Pip is Python's installer, and this one line grabs the toolkit. The stock recipe fine-tunes with just three gigabytes of V RAM, your graphics card's memory."
        },
        {
          "id": "s05",
          "role": "explain",
          "est_duration_s": 12.4,
          "specific": true,
          "hits": [
            "num:about 125 GB of memory at 1-bit, or 300 GB at 4-bit",
            "entity:LoRA",
            "entity:load_in_4bit",
            "entity:True"
          ],
          "narration": "Move two: load a four-bit Llama and attach LoRA. Four bit squeezes each weight to a quarter of its size, about a four-times memory cut. LoRA adds thin trainable layers while the base model stays frozen."
        },
        {
          "id": "s06",
          "role": "explain",
          "est_duration_s": 12.4,
          "specific": true,
          "hits": [
            "entity:Unsloth",
            "entity:LoRA"
          ],
          "narration": "Think vinyl wrap, not repaint, and the wrap still needs the whole car in your garage. So you pick a model that fits and paste one string. LoRA on a four-bit copy: that is Q LoRA."
        },
        {
          "id": "s07",
          "role": "explain",
          "est_duration_s": 9.7,
          "specific": false,
          "hits": [],
          "narration": "Move three: train on your question-and-answer pairs. Two columns, questions and answers, and the loss, your error score, falls while you watch. Write them yourself or generate them."
        },
        {
          "id": "s08",
          "role": "explain",
          "est_duration_s": 10.7,
          "specific": true,
          "hits": [
            "glossary:GGUF"
          ],
          "narration": "Save the adapter, your small trained file, then export to G G U F, the one file Ollama runs. Ollama, LM Studio and llama dot C P P all read it."
        },
        {
          "id": "s09",
          "role": "payoff_close",
          "est_duration_s": 6.9,
          "specific": false,
          "hits": [],
          "narration": "Ollama loads it and you ask your question. The answer comes back in your voice: your fine-tuned Llama, running tonight."
        }
      ]
